<!-- SECTION 1: Introduction -->
<section id="arctan-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find an Angle Using Tangent</h2>
  <p>A right triangle angle from tangent calculator finds an acute angle when you know the two legs of the triangle: the opposite side and the adjacent side. It divides the opposite side by the adjacent side, then applies inverse tangent to that ratio. The result is the angle in degrees.</p>
  <p>Picture a wheelchair ramp. You can measure how high it rises and how far it runs horizontally, but the sloping surface itself might be awkward to measure. Those two measurements, the vertical rise and the horizontal run, are the opposite and adjacent sides. Their ratio is all you need. Inverse tangent converts that ratio directly into the incline angle, with no need to measure the slope length at all.</p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is the angle you want, <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the opposite side, and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the adjacent side. Tangent relates them as <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A) = a/b]]')} />, and inverse tangent reverses the relationship: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arctan(a/b)]]')} />. Because the hypotenuse never enters the formula, this is the natural method whenever the two legs are the measurements you actually have.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: Understanding the sides -->
<section id="arctan-sides" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Opposite and Adjacent Sides</h2>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has one angle of exactly 90 degrees. The other two angles are acute, meaning each is less than 90 degrees, and together they always add up to 90 degrees. The 90-degree condition is what makes the trigonometric ratios work: sine, cosine, and tangent are defined by the fixed relationships between the sides of a right triangle. Without the right angle, the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a/b]]')} /> would not correspond to a single consistent angle.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Opposite Side?</h3>
  <p>The opposite side is the leg that lies directly across from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. It never touches the angle itself. In practical terms it often represents a vertical rise: the height a ladder reaches up a wall, the elevation a roof gains, or the vertical component of a crane boom. The label "opposite" depends on which angle you chose as the reference. If you switch the reference from <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, the side that was opposite becomes adjacent.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Adjacent Side?</h3>
  <p>The adjacent side is the leg that touches angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> but is not the hypotenuse. It forms one edge of the angle. In field measurements it often represents a horizontal run: the ground distance from a wall to a ladder base, the horizontal span of a roof, or the run of a ramp. It is one of the two perpendicular legs, and it must not be confused with the hypotenuse, which is the side opposite the right angle.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse, labelled <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, is the side opposite the 90-degree angle and always the longest side of the triangle. The tangent calculator does not require its length. If you ever need it, the Pythagorean theorem gives it from the two legs: <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}]]')} />. But for finding the angle itself, the hypotenuse is extra information you can simply skip.</p>

  <h3>Why the Reference Angle Matters</h3>
  <p>Opposite and adjacent are defined relative to the chosen angle. In the same triangle, swapping the reference from <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> exchanges the roles of the two legs: the side that was opposite becomes adjacent and vice versa. Reversing the ratio from <span set:html={rich('[[a/b]]')} /> to <span set:html={rich('[[b/a]]')} /> therefore computes the complementary angle <span set:html={rich('[[B = 90^\\circ - A]]')} /> rather than <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. Keeping the reference angle fixed throughout a calculation avoids this mix-up.</p>

