<!-- SECTION 3: Understanding tangent -->
<section id="arctan-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is Tangent in a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Tangent compares the two legs of a right triangle. For angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, it is the opposite side divided by the adjacent side:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\tan(A) = \\frac{a}{b}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)]]')} /> is the tangent of angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the opposite side, and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the adjacent side. Tangent describes how steep the triangle is: it is the ratio of the vertical leg to the horizontal leg when the triangle sits with the adjacent side as its base.</p>
  <p>For example, when <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\tan(A) = \\frac{3}{4} = 0.75]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The ratio has no physical unit. Metres divided by metres cancel out, so whether you measured in metres, feet, or centimetres, the ratio 0.75 is the same pure number.</p>

  <h3>What Is Inverse Tangent?</h3>
  <p>Ordinary tangent starts with an angle and produces a ratio. Inverse tangent starts with a ratio and returns the angle. If <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A) = 0.75]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arctan(0.75) \\approx 36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Inverse tangent reverses the tangent relationship, recovering the original angle from the side ratio.</p>

  <h3>Is Arctangent the Same as Inverse Tangent?</h3>
  <p>Yes. <span set:html={rich('[[\\arctan(x)]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan^{-1}(x)]]')} /> are two notations for the same function. The superscript <span set:html={rich('[[-1]]')} /> does not mean the reciprocal <span set:html={rich('[[1/\\tan(x)]]')} />. The reciprocal of tangent is cotangent, a separate function.</p>

  <h3>Understanding SOH-CAH-TOA</h3>
  <p>SOH-CAH-TOA is the standard memory aid: Sine = Opposite / Hypotenuse, Cosine = Adjacent / Hypotenuse, Tangent = Opposite / Adjacent. The TOA part identifies the correct ratio for this calculator, since the known measurements are the opposite and adjacent sides.</p>

  <h3>Why Tangent Does Not Require the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Tangent uses the two perpendicular legs directly, so the diagonal length is irrelevant to the calculation. Knowing the rise and run is enough to pin down the incline angle. Scaling both legs by the same positive factor leaves the ratio, and therefore the angle, unchanged: a 3-4 triangle and a 6-8 triangle have the same 36.87-degree angle.</p>

