<!-- SECTION 5: Deriving the formula -->
<section id="arctan-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Inverse Tangent Formula Works</h2>
  <p>Start with the definition of tangent:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\tan(A) = \\frac{a}{b}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The known side lengths let you compute the ratio on the right. To recover the angle, apply inverse tangent to both sides:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Inverse tangent is applied to the entire ratio because the ratio as a whole is the tangent of the angle. You cannot split it across the numerator and denominator.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example: Opposite Side 3 and Adjacent Side 4</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p>Step 1: Write the formula. <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arctan(a/b)]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 2: Substitute the values. <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arctan(3/4)]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 3: Calculate the ratio. <span set:html={rich('[[3/4 = 0.75]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 4: Apply inverse tangent. <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arctan(0.75)]]')} /></p>
  <p>Final answer: <span set:html={rich('[[A \\approx 36.87^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The angle is approximately 36.87 degrees. The two decimal places reflect ordinary rounding of an irrational result, not an exact closed form.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="arctan-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Angle From Tangent Calculator</h2>
  <p>Follow these steps to match the live interface:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the right angle.</strong> Confirm the triangle has a 90-degree corner before using any trigonometric ratio.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the reference angle A</strong> you want to calculate.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the opposite side a</strong>, the leg directly across from angle A.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the adjacent side b</strong>, the leg that touches angle A and is not the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure or obtain both side lengths</strong> using the same unit.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the opposite-side length</strong> into the corresponding field.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the adjacent-side length</strong> into its field.</li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible units</strong> with the unit selectors provided by the calculator.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that both side lengths are positive</strong> and greater than zero.</li>
    <li><strong>Press Calculate Angle A</strong> to run the computation.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the calculated angle</strong> in degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Check whether the result makes sense</strong> for the triangle: a ratio near 1 should give an angle near 45 degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset</strong> to clear the inputs and start a new calculation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Each step matters because a mislabeled side is the most common source of a wrong answer. Taking a moment to confirm which leg is opposite and which is adjacent prevents the most frequent error.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 7: Four foundational examples -->
<section id="arctan-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Finding an Angle Using Tangent</h2>
  <p>These four examples build the core skill before the real-world applications. Each follows the same pattern: write the formula, substitute, divide, apply arctan.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: A 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> cm and adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4]]')} /> cm, find angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Step 1: Write the formula. <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arctan(a/b)]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 2: Substitute. <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arctan(3/4)]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 3: Calculate. <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arctan(0.75)]]')} /></p>
  <p>Final answer: <span set:html={rich('[[A \\approx 36.87^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The hypotenuse is not needed even though this is a 3-4-5 triangle. The two legs alone determine the angle, which is why arctan is the efficient choice here.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: Equal Opposite and Adjacent Sides</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 5]]')} /> m:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{5}{5}\\right) = \\arctan(1) = 45^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Equal legs produce an isosceles right triangle, so the two acute angles are both exactly 45 degrees.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: An Angle Below 45 Degrees</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 6]]')} /> m:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) \\approx 26.57^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The angle is less than 45 degrees because the opposite side is shorter than the adjacent side. A short rise over a long run makes a shallow angle.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: An Angle Above 45 Degrees</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 8]]')} /> ft and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4]]')} /> ft:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{8}{4}\\right) \\approx 63.43^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The angle exceeds 45 degrees because the opposite side is longer than the adjacent side. A tall rise over a short run makes a steep angle.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 8: Real-world applications -->
<section id="arctan-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Examples of Calculating Angles Using Tangent</h2>
  <p>Twelve fully worked practical examples. For each one, the situation is described, the triangle identified, the measurements stated, the correct sides named, the formula written and evaluated, and the result interpreted with its practical limits.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Calculating a Roof Pitch Angle</h3>
  <p>A roof rises 4 ft vertically over a horizontal run of 12 ft. Find the angle between the roof slope and the horizontal.</p>
  <p>Known: opposite rise <span set:html={rich('[[a = 4]]')} /> ft, adjacent run <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} /> ft. Inverse tangent is appropriate because the two perpendicular measurements are known and the slope length is not needed.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{4}{12}\\right) \\approx 18.43^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The roof rises at about 18.43 degrees above the horizontal. Note that a roof pitch expressed as 4:12 is a ratio, not an angle of 4 degrees. This geometric result is not a structural assessment.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Finding the Angle of a Ladder</h3>
  <p>A ladder reaches 4.5 m up a wall, and its base is 2 m from the wall. Find the angle between the ladder and the ground.</p>
  <p>Known: opposite height <span set:html={rich('[[a = 4.5]]')} /> m, adjacent ground distance <span set:html={rich('[[b = 2]]')} /> m.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{4.5}{2}\\right) \\approx 66.04^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The wall height is opposite the ground angle and the ground distance is adjacent. The calculated angle alone does not establish whether the ladder is safely positioned.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Calculating a Ramp Incline Angle</h3>
  <p>A ramp rises 0.75 m over a horizontal run of 9 m. Find the incline angle.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{0.75}{9}\\right) \\approx 4.76^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical rise and horizontal run are the correct tangent inputs. This angle does not by itself confirm accessibility or building-code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: Estimating the Angle of Elevation to a Tree</h3>
  <p>A person stands 20 m from a tree. The top of the tree is 12 m above the observation level. Calculate the angle of elevation.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{12}{20}\\right) \\approx 30.96^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical height difference is the opposite side and the horizontal distance is the adjacent side. The result is the angle above the horizontal from the observation point, not the tree total height.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: A Surveying Elevation Measurement</h3>
  <p>A simplified surveying sketch records a vertical difference of 7 m and a horizontal distance of 12 m. Calculate the angle of elevation.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right) \\approx 30.26^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The elevation difference and horizontal distance form the opposite and adjacent sides. Real surveying may require instrument-height corrections and terrain adjustments beyond this simplified model.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Finding a Mechanical Support Angle</h3>
  <p>A mechanical support has a vertical rise of 1.2 m and a horizontal run of 3.5 m. Calculate the angle relative to the horizontal.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{1.2}{3.5}\\right) \\approx 18.92^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Inverse tangent converts the two perpendicular measurements into an angle without needing the diagonal length. The calculation does not establish the support strength or suitability.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Calculating a Staircase Incline</h3>
  <p>A simplified staircase rises 2.8 m over a horizontal run of 4 m. Calculate the overall incline angle.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{2.8}{4}\\right) \\approx 34.99^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The total vertical rise and horizontal run determine the overall angle. This simplified calculation does not verify building-code compliance or individual stair dimensions.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: Finding a Guy Wire Angle</h3>
  <p>A guy wire connects two points with a vertical separation of 10 m and a horizontal separation of 15 m. Find the angle between the wire and the horizontal.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{10}{15}\\right) \\approx 33.69^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical separation is opposite the angle and the horizontal projection is adjacent. The angle calculation does not determine wire tension, anchor strength, or structural safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 13: Calculating a Road Grade Angle</h3>
  <p>A road rises 5 m over a horizontal distance of 100 m. Find the road incline angle.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{5}{100}\\right) \\approx 2.86^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The percentage grade is <span set:html={rich('[[(5/100) \\times 100 = 5\\%]]')} />. A 5 percent grade is not a 5-degree angle; the angle is arctan of the ratio, about 2.86 degrees.</p>

  <h3>Example 14: Finding a Crane Boom Angle</h3>
  <p>A simplified boom has a vertical rise of 8 m and a horizontal projection of 6 m. Find the angle relative to the horizontal.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{8}{6}\\right) \\approx 53.13^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical projection is opposite and the horizontal projection is adjacent. This does not determine crane capacity or operational safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 15: Calculating a Hiking Trail Incline</h3>
  <p>A straight section of a hiking trail rises 80 m over a horizontal run of 150 m. Find the angle of incline.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{80}{150}\\right) \\approx 28.07^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The calculation uses vertical elevation gain as the opposite side and horizontal distance as the adjacent side. Real trails vary in gradient rather than forming one straight incline.</p>

  <h3>Example 16: Finding an Awning Support Angle</h3>
  <p>An awning support has a vertical rise of 1.2 m and a horizontal run of 2.4 m. Calculate the angle relative to the horizontal.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{1.2}{2.4}\\right) \\approx 26.57^\\circ]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The ratio determines the angle without needing the diagonal support length. The result does not establish the support structural suitability.</p>

