<!-- SECTION 10: Degrees, units, rounding -->
<section id="arctan-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Angle Units and Measurement Precision</h2>
  <p>The calculator returns degrees. A degree is 1/360 of a full rotation. A radian is the alternative angular unit, defined so that a full rotation equals <span set:html={rich('[[2\\pi]]')} /> radians. Inverse tangent can return either unit depending on the calculator, but this tool reports degrees.</p>
  <p>The ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a/b]]')} /> itself has no unit, and the two sides must use compatible units. Converting both measurements from metres to centimetres multiplies numerator and denominator by 100, leaving the ratio unchanged.</p>
  <p>To convert radians to degrees: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{Degrees} = \\text{Radians} \\times 180^\\circ / \\pi]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Example: Does the Measurement Unit Change the Angle?</h3>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4]]')} /> m. The equivalent in centimetres is <span set:html={rich('[[a = 300]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 400]]')} /> cm. Both give the ratio 0.75 and the same angle of 36.87 degrees. Keep enough precision in intermediate steps and round the final angle to match the accuracy of the original measurements.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 11: Common mistakes -->
<section id="arctan-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating an Angle Using Tangent</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Using the hypotenuse instead of the adjacent side.</strong> The formula needs the leg touching angle A, not the longest side. Check: is your denominator one of the two legs?</li>
    <li><strong>Swapping the opposite and adjacent sides.</strong> This computes the complementary angle instead. If you expected about 37 degrees and got about 53, the legs are swapped.</li>
    <li><strong>Reversing the ratio into b/a.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[\\arctan(b/a)]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. For a 3-4 triangle, arctan(4/3) is 53.13 degrees, the other acute angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Using ordinary tangent instead of inverse tangent.</strong> Typing tan(0.75) instead of arctan(0.75) answers a different question entirely.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing inverse tangent with reciprocal tangent.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan^{-1}(x)]]')} /> is not <span set:html={rich('[[1/\\tan(x)]]')} />. The reciprocal is cotangent.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming the tangent ratio must be between 0 and 1.</strong> It can exceed 1 freely. A ratio of 2 with angle 63.43 degrees is perfectly valid.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering zero or negative side lengths.</strong> Side lengths must be positive. Zero or negative inputs have no geometric meaning here.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing incompatible measurement units.</strong> Metres for one leg and feet for the other corrupts the ratio. Convert first.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding the ratio too early.</strong> Rounding 0.6667 to 0.67 before applying arctan shifts the answer. Keep full precision until the final step.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting radians when degrees are expected.</strong> Arctan(1) is 45 degrees but 0.785 radians. Check your calculator mode.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing angle A with its complementary angle B.</strong> Remember <span set:html={rich('[[A + B = 90^\\circ]]')} />. If the answer looks like the "other" angle, the ratio was inverted.</li>
    <li><strong>Treating a percentage grade as an angle in degrees.</strong> A 5% grade is arctan(0.05), about 2.86 degrees, not 5 degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing vertical rise with sloping distance.</strong> The opposite side is the pure vertical component, not the length along the slope.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming the calculated angle proves a structure is safe.</strong> Geometry describes shape only. Ladder safety, roof loads, and ramp compliance need separate engineering checks.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 12: Verification -->
<section id="arctan-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Calculated Angle Is Valid</h2>
  <p>Valid inputs for a non-degenerate right triangle: the opposite side must be positive, the adjacent side must be positive, neither can be zero, both must use compatible units, and the resulting angle must fall strictly between 0 and 90 degrees. Unlike sine and cosine, the tangent ratio is not restricted to values below 1, so an opposite side longer than the adjacent side is fine.</p>

  <h3>Method 1: Check the Tangent Ratio</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A) \\approx a/b]]')} /> using the unrounded angle when possible. If tan of your answer reproduces the input ratio, the answer is consistent.</p>

  <h3>Method 2: Check the Complementary Angle</h3>
  <p>Calculate <span set:html={rich('[[B = 90^\\circ - A]]')} />. The two acute angles of a right triangle must sum to 90 degrees, so this is a quick sanity check on every result.</p>

  <h3>Method 3: Use the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Compute the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}]]')} />, then confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Full check with <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5]]')} />. The angle <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arctan(3/4) \\approx 36.87^\\circ]]')} /> and its complement <span set:html={rich('[[B \\approx 53.13^\\circ]]')} /> sum to 90 degrees. Small differences come from rounding the displayed angle.</p>
</section>

