<section id="arctan-choose" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Choosing the Right Right Triangle Calculator</h2>
  <p>Match the tool to the measurements you actually have:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Angle From Tangent Calculator</strong> (this page): opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> known, finds angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. Best when both legs were measured directly.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-angle-from-sine-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Angle From Sine Calculator</strong></a>: opposite side and hypotenuse known. Best for height problems where the sloping length was measured.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-angle-from-cosine-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Angle From Cosine Calculator</strong></a>: adjacent side and hypotenuse known. Best when the hypotenuse was measured directly along a slope.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-angle-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Angle Calculator</strong></a>: helps you pick the right method when you are unsure which sides you have.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-missing-angle-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Missing Angle Calculator</strong></a>: finds the complementary angle when one acute angle is already known.</li>
    <li><a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline"><strong>Side Calculator</strong></a>: finds missing side lengths rather than angles.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>All three inverse functions agree on the same triangle: for a 3-4-5 triangle, arctan(3/4), arcsin(3/5), and arccos(4/5) all give 36.87 degrees. If two methods disagree, a side is mislabeled.</p>
